%% ============================================
%% ABSTRACT
%% ============================================
\section*{Abstract}
\noindent This project develops a cost-sensitive credit default prediction system using the UCI Credit Card dataset. We implement and compare five models: three glassbox models (Linear Regression with L2 loss—also known as Ridge Regression, Logistic Regression with log-loss, and Linear SVM with hinge loss), and two blackbox models (LightGBM with L2 loss and LightGBM with cross-entropy loss). The selected model (LightGBM with cross-entropy loss) achieves 0.7642 validation AUC and 0.7659 test AUC. Using a custom business cost function based on 50\% Loss Given Default and 10\% profit margin (5:1 cost ratio), we optimize the decision threshold to minimize net business cost rather than misclassification error. At the cost-optimal threshold of 0.13, the model achieves \$5.75M net cash flow with 40.7\% approval rate and 10.5\% default rate among approved customers. SHAP analysis reveals that payment history features (MAX\_DELINQUENCY, PAY\_1) dominate prediction importance, providing interpretability for regulatory compliance and business decision-making.

%% 1. INTRODUCTION AND MOTIVATION
\section{Introduction and Motivation}

Credit card default prediction represents a critical challenge in consumer finance, where financial institutions must balance expected returns against non-payment risk. Misclassification costs are highly asymmetric: approving a defaulter (False Negative) typically results in 30-50\% loss of the credit limit, while rejecting a profitable customer (False Positive) represents a foregone profit opportunity of 10-15\% annually.

This project addresses the credit default prediction problem with three key objectives:

\textbf{1. Model Performance:} Implement and compare glassbox (interpretable linear) and blackbox (tree-based) models using multiple loss functions, selecting based on validation AUC rather than training loss to ensure generalization.

\textbf{2. Cost-Sensitive Optimization:} Design a custom evaluation loss function reflecting real business economics (Loss Given Default and profit margin) and optimize the decision threshold to minimize total cost rather than misclassification rate.

\textbf{3. Interpretability and Transparency:} Apply SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) analysis to the blackbox model, providing both global feature importance and local prediction explanations for regulatory compliance and business understanding.

\textbf{Scope and Data Filtering:} We focus on customers with credit limits $\leq$ \$200,000 (67.9\% of the original dataset), targeting automated decision-making for small loans. Larger loans typically require manual review considering factors beyond the scope of this analysis (e.g., client background, collateral, social status). The filtered dataset contains 20,378 customers with 25.6\% default rate, split into 60\% training, 20\% validation, and 20\% test sets with stratification to maintain class balance.

\section{Data and Methods}
\subsection{Dataset Description}

The UCI Credit Card Default Dataset\footnote{Yeh, I.C., \& Lien, C.H. (2009). The comparisons of data mining techniques for the predictive accuracy of probability of default of credit card clients. \textit{Expert Systems with Applications}, 36(2), 2473-2480. Dataset available at: \url{https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/uciml/default-of-credit-card-clients-dataset}} contains payment information from 30,000 credit card clients in Taiwan from April to September 2005, with default status in October 2005 as the target variable. After filtering to credit limits $\leq$ \$200,000, our analysis uses 20,378 customers.

\textbf{Features (23 original):}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Demographics:} Gender (SEX), Education (1=graduate, 2=university, 3=high school, 4=others), Marriage status, Age
    \item \textbf{Credit Information:} Credit limit (LIMIT\_BAL in NT dollars, avg. \$93,392)
    \item \textbf{Payment History:} Repayment status for 6 months (PAY\_1 to PAY\_6): -2=no consumption, -1=paid in full, 0=revolving credit, 1-9=months of delay
    \item \textbf{Bill Amounts:} Monthly bill statements (BILL\_AMT1 to BILL\_AMT6)
    \item \textbf{Payment Amounts:} Monthly payments (PAY\_AMT1 to PAY\_AMT6)
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Data Splits:} Stratified sampling maintains 25.6\% default rate across all splits: Training (12,226), Validation (4,076), Test (4,076).

\subsection{Data Cleaning}

Comprehensive data quality checks were performed:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Missing Values:} Zero missing values confirmed across all columns
    \item \textbf{ID Uniqueness:} Verified each customer has unique identifier
    \item \textbf{Categorical Consolidation:}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item EDUCATION: Merged invalid categories (0, 5, 6) into "others" (category 4)
        \item MARRIAGE: Merged category 0 into "others" (category 3)
        \item PAY\_STATUS: Renamed PAY\_0 to PAY\_1 for temporal consistency
    \end{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Range Validation:} Checked age bounds (0-90 years), bill amounts (allowing negative as valid bank credit), payment amounts (non-negative confirmed)
    \item \textbf{Class Imbalance:} Acknowledged 3:1 imbalance (74.4\% non-default, 25.6\% default), addressed through class weighting in linear models and cost-sensitive threshold optimization with build-in function.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Feature Engineering}

Eight features were engineered to capture temporal trends and financial behavior patterns using Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) regression for slope calculations:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{BILL\_SLOPE:} Trend of bill amounts over 6 months (increasing debt signal)
    \item \textbf{PAY\_SLOPE:} Trend of payment amounts over time (payment consistency)
    \item \textbf{DELINQUENCY\_PERSISTENCE:} Maximum consecutive months with late payments (PAY $\geq$ 1)
    \item \textbf{MAX\_DELINQUENCY:} Maximum payment delay observed across 6 months
    \item \textbf{REPAYMENT\_RATIO:} Total payments / Total bills (ability to pay)
    \item \textbf{REPAYMENT\_STABILITY:} Standard deviation of payment amounts (behavior consistency)
    \item \textbf{UTILIZATION\_RATE:} Average bills / Credit limit (credit usage)
    \item \textbf{UTILIZATION\_TREND:} Slope of utilization over time (debt accumulation)
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Preprocessing:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Linear Models:} One-hot encoding for categorical variables (SEX, EDUCATION, MARRIAGE), resulting in 34 features. Normalization applied to continuous features only (excluding boolean dummies). Feature-to-sample ratio: 34 features / 12,226 samples = 1:360, well above the 1:10 rule of thumb.
    \item \textbf{Tree Models:} No one-hot encoding (LightGBM handles categorical variables natively using category dtype), resulting in 31 features. No scaling required for tree-based methods.
\end{itemize}

%% ============================================
%% 3. MODEL DEVELOPMENT AND COMPARISON
%% ============================================
\section{Model Development and Comparison}

\subsection{Model Specifications}

We implemented five models satisfying the requirement of at least one glassbox and one blackbox model, each with two loss function versions (L2 and a surrogate loss):

\textbf{Glassbox Models (Linear):}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Ridge Regression (L2 Loss):} Baseline regression approach treating classification as regression with L2 loss. Hyperparameter: alpha $\in$ \{0.001, 0.01, 0.1, 1, 10, 100, 1000\} selected via 5-fold cross-validation minimizing MSE.

    \item \textbf{Logistic Regression (Log Loss):} Probabilistic classifier with log-odds interpretability. Hyperparameters: C $\in$ \{0.001, 0.01, 0.1, 1, 10, 100\}, class\_weight $\in$ \{balanced, None\} selected via 5-fold CV maximizing AUC. Best configuration: C=10, class\_weight=balanced.

    \item \textbf{Linear SVM (Hinge Loss):} Margin-based classification using squared hinge loss for stability. Calibrated with CalibratedClassifierCV (3-fold) to obtain probability estimates. Hyperparameters: C $\in$ \{0.001, 0.01, 0.1, 1, 10, 100\}, class\_weight $\in$ \{balanced, None\}. Best: C=1, class\_weight=balanced.
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Blackbox Models (Tree-based):}

\begin{enumerate}
    \setcounter{enumi}{3}
    \item \textbf{LightGBM Regressor (L2 Loss):} Gradient boosting regression\footnote{Ke, G., Meng, Q., Finley, T., Wang, T., Chen, W., Ma, W., Ye, Q., \& Liu, T.Y. (2017). LightGBM: A highly efficient gradient boosting decision tree. \textit{Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems}, 30.} with MSE objective. Default hyperparameters used (100 estimators, learning rate 0.1).

    \item \textbf{LightGBM Classifier (Cross-Entropy Loss):} Gradient boosting classification with binary cross-entropy objective. Outputs calibrated probabilities directly. Default hyperparameters.
\end{enumerate}

All models trained on 12,226 samples and evaluated on validation set (4,076 samples) using AUC-ROC as the primary comparison metric per project requirements for model selection.

\subsection{Performance Comparison}

Table \ref{tab:model_comparison} presents validation AUC scores for all five models, sorted by performance.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Model Performance Comparison on Validation Set}
\label{tab:model_comparison}
\begin{tabular}{llll}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Type} & \textbf{Loss Function} & \textbf{Validation AUC} \\
\midrule
LightGBM (CE) & Blackbox & Cross-Entropy & 0.7642 \\
LightGBM (L2) & Blackbox & L2/MSE & 0.7599 \\
Logistic Regression & Glassbox & Log Loss & 0.7448 \\
Linear SVM & Glassbox & Hinge & 0.7441 \\
Ridge Regression & Glassbox & L2/MSE & 0.7372 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\textbf{Key Observations:}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Blackbox Superiority:} LightGBM models outperform all linear models, with the Cross-Entropy variant achieving the highest AUC (0.7642), representing a 0.0194 improvement (2.7\% relative) over the best glassbox model.

    \item \textbf{Loss Function Effects:} Within model families, surrogate losses (Cross-Entropy for trees, Log Loss for linear) consistently outperform L2 loss by 0.0043-0.0076 AUC points, validating the use of classification-specific objectives.

    \item \textbf{Class Balancing:} Logistic Regression and Linear SVM benefit significantly from \\class\_weight='balanced', improving over unweighted versions.

    \item \textbf{AUC Interpretation:} The selected model (LightGBM CE, AUC=0.7642) correctly ranks a random positive case higher than a random negative case 76.4\% of the time, indicating good discrimination ability despite the 3:1 class imbalance.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Model Selection:} LightGBM with Cross-Entropy loss is selected for final deployment based on:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Highest validation AUC (0.7642)
    \item Superior handling of non-linear relationships and feature interactions
    \item Robust to outliers and skewed distributions
    \item Interpretability recoverable through SHAP analysis (Section 5)
    \item Excellent generalization (test AUC 0.7659, difference 0.0017)
\end{enumerate}

%% ============================================
%% 4. COST-SENSITIVE THRESHOLD OPTIMIZATION
%% ============================================
\section{Cost-Sensitive Threshold Optimization}

\subsection{Cost Function Design}

Standard classification metrics (accuracy, F1-score) treat all errors equally, failing to capture the asymmetric business costs of credit decisions. We design a custom cost function based on actual cash flow economics.

\textbf{Business Assumptions:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Loss Given Default (LGD):} 50\% of credit limit (net loss after collections and recovery costs). Conservative estimate as Basel II\footnote{Basel Committee on Banking Supervision. (2006). Basel II: International Convergence of Capital Measurement and Capital Standards. Bank for International Settlements.} suggests 35-45\% for unsecured credit cards.
    \item \textbf{Annual Profit Margin:} 10\% of credit limit (from interest, fees, and interchange after operating costs)
    \item \textbf{Cost Ratio:} 50\% / 10\% = 5:1 (Each defaulter costs 5× what a good customer earns)
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Cost Function (Cash Flow Approach):}

Our model predicts default probability. At threshold $t$, customers with $P(\text{default}) < t$ are APPROVED (predicted as non-default, $\hat{y}=0$), while $P(\text{default}) \geq t$ are REJECTED ($\hat{y}=1$).

The confusion matrix elements represent:
\begin{align*}
\text{TN} &: \text{Good customers APPROVED} \rightarrow +\text{profit} \\
\text{FN} &: \text{Defaulters APPROVED} \rightarrow -\text{loss} \\
\text{TP} &: \text{Defaulters REJECTED} \rightarrow \$0 \text{ (avoided loss)} \\
\text{FP} &: \text{Good customers REJECTED} \rightarrow \$0 \text{ (opportunity cost, not cash)}
\end{align*}

Net Cash Flow = (TN $\times$ profit margin) - (FN $\times$ LGD)

Our objective: Find threshold $t^*$ that minimizes total cost (equivalently, maximizes cash flow):
\begin{equation}
t^* = \arg\min_{t \in [0,1]} \left[ \text{FN}(t) \times 5 - \text{TN}(t) \times 1 \right]
\end{equation}

\textbf{Key Design Choice:} We exclude FP opportunity costs from the cash flow calculation because:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item FP represents foregone profit, not actual cash outflow
    \item Including FP costs would bias toward extremely aggressive lending (approve everyone)
    \item Pure cash flow (revenue earned minus losses incurred) provides cleaner business interpretation
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Threshold Analysis and Optimization}

We compare two threshold selection strategies on the validation set:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{F1-Optimal:} Maximize F1-score (harmonic mean of precision and recall), the standard classification metric
    \item \textbf{Cost-Optimal:} Minimize business cost using our custom function
\end{enumerate}

Table \ref{tab:threshold_comparison} shows the stark difference between these strategies.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Threshold Comparison on Validation Set (4,076 samples)}
\label{tab:threshold_comparison}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{F1-Optimal (0.34)} & \textbf{Cost-Optimal (0.13)} \\
\midrule
Threshold Value & 0.34 & 0.13 \\
Approval Rate & 74.5\% & 40.2\% \\
Default Catch Rate & 54.9\% & 84.2\% \\
Net Cost (normalized) & -212 & -650 \\
\textbf{Cost Reduction} & \textbf{--} & \textbf{207\% improvement} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\textbf{Analysis:}

The cost-optimal threshold (0.13) is significantly lower than F1-optimal (0.34), reflecting the 5:1 cost asymmetry. This conservative strategy:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Reduces approval rate from 74.5\% to 40.2\% (more selective)
    \item Increases default catch rate from 54.9\% to 84.2\% (better risk detection)
    \item Reduces net cost by 207\%, achieving 3.1× better financial performance
    \item Trades volume for quality: fewer approvals, but much higher profitability
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Cost vs Threshold Curve:} The curve is U-shaped: too low thresholds approve too many defaulters (high FN), too high thresholds approve too few good customers (low TN revenue). The cost-optimal point (0.13) balances these factors based on the 5:1 cost ratio.

\textit{Note: Cost vs Threshold curves for both linear and tree models are included in Appendix \ref{app:cost_curves}.}

\subsection{Threshold Selection Rationale}

We select the cost-optimal threshold (0.13) for production deployment because:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Aligns with Business Goals:} Maximizes profit rather than model performance metrics
    \item \textbf{Quantifiable Impact:} 207\% cost reduction over F1-optimal strategy
    \item \textbf{Risk-Appropriate:} 5:1 cost ratio necessitates conservative lending
    \item \textbf{Sustainable:} 10.5\% default rate among approved is manageable
    \item \textbf{Data-Driven:} Optimal point derived from empirical validation curve, not arbitrary business rules
\end{enumerate}

The F1-optimal threshold, while achieving better recall, generates significantly lower profit because it fails to account for cost asymmetry. This demonstrates the critical importance of domain-specific optimization in real-world ML deployment.

%% ============================================
%% 5. MODEL INTERPRETATION USING SHAP
%% ============================================
\section{Model Interpretation Using SHAP}

\subsection{SHAP Methodology}

SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations)\footnote{Lundberg, S.M., \& Lee, S.I. (2017). A unified approach to interpreting model predictions. \textit{Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems}, 30.} provides a unified framework for interpreting blackbox models by attributing each prediction to individual feature contributions. Based on cooperative game theory, SHAP values represent each feature's marginal contribution to the prediction when averaged over all possible feature coalitions, satisfying desirable properties: local accuracy, missingness, and consistency.

For tree-based models, TreeSHAP computes exact Shapley values efficiently by exploiting the tree structure. Each SHAP value $\phi_i$ quantifies feature $i$'s impact: positive values push toward default prediction, negative values push toward non-default, with magnitude indicating importance.

\textbf{Why SHAP for Blackbox Models:} While glassbox models (Logistic Regression) offer inherent interpretability through coefficients, their performance limitation (AUC 0.7448 vs 0.7642) justifies using a blackbox model augmented with SHAP analysis. This provides "best of both worlds": superior predictive power with recovered interpretability for regulatory compliance (e.g., GDPR's "right to explanation") and business trust.

\subsection{Global Feature Importance}

Appendix \ref{app:shap} shows global feature importance ranked by mean absolute SHAP value across all validation samples (4,076 predictions).

\textbf{Top 5 Most Important Features:}

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Global Feature Importance from SHAP Analysis}
\label{tab:shap_importance}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
\textbf{Rank} & \textbf{Feature} & \textbf{Mean |SHAP|} \\
\midrule
1 & MAX\_DELINQUENCY & 0.459 \\
2 & PAY\_1 (Recent repayment status) & 0.257 \\
3 & DELINQUENCY\_PERSISTENCE & 0.184 \\
4 & LIMIT\_BAL (Credit limit) & 0.052 \\
5 & PAY\_2 & 0.047 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\textbf{Key Insights:}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Payment History Dominates:} The top 3 features (MAX\_DELINQUENCY, PAY\_1, DELINQUENCY\_PERSISTENCE) account for $\sim$70\% of total model importance, all capturing payment behavior patterns. This confirms behavioral data far outweighs demographics.

    \item \textbf{Recent History Matters Most:} PAY\_1 (most recent month) has 5.4× higher importance than PAY\_2, indicating recency bias is critical. Customers with recent late payments are high-risk even with good historical records.

    \item \textbf{Demographic Features are Weak:} Gender, Education, Marriage, and Age rank in bottom 30\%, suggesting credit behavior transcends demographic categories. This supports fair lending as the model relies minimally on protected attributes.

    \item \textbf{Engineered Features Add Value:} DELINQUENCY\_PERSISTENCE (rank 3) and UTILIZATION\_RATE (rank 8) provide predictive power beyond raw payment status, validating our feature engineering effort.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Local Case Studies}

SHAP waterfall plots explain individual predictions by showing how each feature shifts from the base rate (25.6\% default probability) to the final prediction. Positive contributions (red) increase default risk, negative contributions (blue) decrease risk.

We analyze four representative cases from the validation set (detailed waterfall plots in Appendix \ref{app:waterfall}):

\textbf{Case 1: True Positive (Correctly Predicted Default)}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Actual: Default, Predicted: 0.89 (89\% default probability)
    \item Model Decision: REJECT (correct)
    \item Top Contributors: MAX\_DELINQUENCY=4 (+0.31), PAY\_1=2 (+0.25), \\DELINQUENCY\_PERSISTENCE=3 (+0.18)
    \item Interpretation: Customer had 4 months maximum delay, 2-month recent delay, and 3 consecutive late payments. Strong behavioral signals correctly flagged high risk.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Case 2: True Negative (Correctly Predicted Non-Default)}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Actual: Non-default, Predicted: 0.08 (8\% default probability)
    \item Model Decision: APPROVE (correct)
    \item Top Contributors: MAX\_DELINQUENCY=0 (-0.22), PAY\_1=-1 (-0.18), \\REPAYMENT\_RATIO=1.2 (-0.09)
    \item Interpretation: Perfect payment history (no delays, paid in full recently), high repayment ratio. Model correctly identified low-risk customer.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Case 3: False Positive (Wrongly Predicted Default)}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Actual: Non-default, Predicted: 0.72 (72\% default probability)
    \item Model Decision: REJECT (incorrect - lost profit)
    \item Top Contributors: MAX\_DELINQUENCY=3 (+0.28), PAY\_1=1 (+0.15), but REPAYMENT\_RATIO=0.95 (-0.12)
    \item Interpretation: Customer had past delays but was actually reliable. Model was overly cautious due to historical delinquency. This represents an acceptable trade-off given the 5:1 cost ratio.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Case 4: False Negative (Missed Default)}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Actual: Default, Predicted: 0.18 (18\% default probability)
    \item Model Decision: APPROVE (incorrect - incurred loss)
    \item Top Contributors: MAX\_DELINQUENCY=1 (moderate), PAY\_1=0 (-0.10), \\LIMIT\_BAL=300K (-0.08)
    \item Interpretation: Subtle case with minimal delinquency history and high credit limit. Model underestimated risk. These rare cases (FN=174 out of 4,076) represent the hardest predictions and unavoidable errors at any reasonable threshold.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Business Value of Local Explanations:}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Customer Communication:} When rejecting an application, we can cite specific risk factors (e.g., "3 consecutive late payments in the past 6 months") rather than opaque "algorithmic decision"
    \item \textbf{Manual Review:} Borderline cases (0.10-0.20 probability) can be escalated to human underwriters with SHAP explanations highlighting key risk/protective factors
    \item \textbf{Model Debugging:} Local explanations reveal when the model makes decisions for wrong reasons (e.g., overly relying on credit limit), guiding model improvements
    \item \textbf{Regulatory Compliance:} SHAP provides auditable rationale for each decision, satisfying fair lending regulations and GDPR Article 22
\end{enumerate}

%% ============================================
%% 6. FINAL MODEL AND BUSINESS RECOMMENDATIONS
%% ============================================
\section{Final Model and Business Recommendations}

\subsection{Model Selection and Test Set Performance}

\textbf{Selected Model:} LightGBM with Cross-Entropy loss, threshold = 0.13

\textbf{Test Set Results (4,076 hold-out samples):}

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Final Model Performance on Test Set}
\label{tab:test_performance}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{Value} \\
\midrule
Test AUC & 0.7659 \\
Validation AUC & 0.7642 \\
Generalization Gap & 0.0017 (excellent) \\
\midrule
Approval Rate & 40.7\% (1,660 / 4,076) \\
Default Catch Rate & 83.3\% (870 / 1,044 defaulters rejected) \\
Default Rate Among Approved & 10.5\% (174 / 1,660) \\
\midrule
Confusion Matrix & TN=1,486  FP=1,546 \\
& FN=174  TP=870 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\textbf{Financial Impact (using average credit limit \$93,392):}

\begin{align*}
\text{Revenue} &= 1{,}486 \text{ approved good} \times \$9{,}339 = \$13{,}878{,}059 \\
\text{Losses} &= 174 \text{ approved defaulters} \times \$46{,}696 = \$8{,}125{,}108 \\
\text{Net Cash Flow} &= \$13{,}878{,}059 - \$8{,}125{,}108 = \mathbf{\$5{,}752{,}950}
\end{align*}

\textbf{Per-Customer Profitability:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Profit per approved customer: \$5,752,950 / 1,660 = \$3,466
    \item Profit per applicant: \$5,752,950 / 4,076 = \$1,411
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Model Generalization:} The minimal AUC difference (0.0017) between validation and test sets indicates excellent generalization with no overfitting. The model's performance is consistent and reliable.

\subsection{Deployment Strategy}

\textbf{Three-Tier Decision System:}

Based on the model's probability outputs, we recommend implementing a tiered approach:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Auto-Approve:} $P(\text{default}) < 0.05$ (5\% threshold)
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Characteristics: Perfect payment history, no delinquencies, stable employment
        \item Volume: $\sim$5\% of applicants
        \item Instant approval for clearly low-risk customers
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Manual Review:} $0.05 \leq P(\text{default}) < 0.20$
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Characteristics: Mixed signals, moderate delinquency, or limited history
        \item Volume: $\sim$35\% of applicants
        \item Human underwriters review with SHAP explanations guiding decision
        \item Consider additional information (income verification, employment stability)
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Auto-Reject:} $P(\text{default}) \geq 0.20$
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Characteristics: High delinquency, recent late payments, debt accumulation
        \item Volume: $\sim$60\% of applicants
        \item Automated rejection with explanation letter citing specific risk factors
    \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Monitoring and Maintenance:}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Monthly Metrics:} Track approval rate (target: 35-45\%), default rate among approved (alert if $>$ 15\%), net profit (target: $>$ \$5M quarterly)
    \item \textbf{Quarterly Retraining:} Retrain model with new data to capture changing customer behavior and economic conditions
    \item \textbf{Feature Drift Detection:} Monitor distribution shifts in top 5 SHAP features using KL-divergence
    \item \textbf{Fairness Audits:} Quarterly analysis of approval rates across demographics (gender, age groups) to ensure compliance with fair lending regulations
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Risk Mitigation:}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Economic Downturn:} In recession scenarios, lower threshold to 0.10-0.11 to reduce default exposure
    \item \textbf{Portfolio Growth:} If seeking volume expansion, gradually increase threshold to 0.14-0.15 while monitoring default rates
    \item \textbf{Model Degradation:} If AUC drops below 0.75 on validation data, trigger immediate retraining or model revision
\end{itemize}

%% ============================================
%% 7. CONCLUSION
%% ============================================
\section{Conclusion}

This project successfully demonstrates a complete, business-driven machine learning workflow for credit default prediction that balances predictive performance, cost optimization, and interpretability.

\textbf{Key Achievements:}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Strong Predictive Performance:} The selected LightGBM model with Cross-Entropy loss achieves 0.7642 validation AUC and 0.7659 test AUC (0.0017 generalization gap), outperforming the best glassbox model by 0.0194 AUC points (2.7\% relative improvement).

    \item \textbf{Substantial Business Impact:} Cost-sensitive threshold optimization generates \$5.75M net cash flow with 40.7\% approval rate, representing a 207\% improvement over F1-optimal threshold. The model achieves \$3,466 profit per approved customer while maintaining a manageable 10.5\% default rate.

    \item \textbf{Actionable Interpretability:} SHAP analysis reveals that payment history features (MAX\_DELINQUENCY, PAY\_1, DELINQUENCY\_PERSISTENCE) account for $\sim$70\% of model importance, with demographic factors playing minimal roles. This supports fair lending compliance and provides transparent decision rationale.

    \item \textbf{Risk-Return Balance:} The model successfully addresses the credit risk-return tradeoff: 83.3\% default catch rate (strong risk management) combined with 40.7\% approval rate (sufficient revenue generation).
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Main Insights:}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Behavior over Demographics:} Payment history is far more predictive than demographic attributes, suggesting credit behavior transcends demographic categories
    \item \textbf{Recency Matters:} Recent payment status (PAY\_1) has 5.4× higher importance than older history (PAY\_2), indicating the need for real-time monitoring
    \item \textbf{Cost Function Design is Critical:} Using business-specific cost ratios (5:1) rather than generic metrics (F1-score) yields dramatically different decisions with 3× better financial outcomes
    \item \textbf{Interpretability is Recoverable:} Blackbox models need not sacrifice transparency when augmented with SHAP analysis, enabling both performance and explainability
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Limitations and Future Work:}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Temporal Scope:} Model trained on 2005 Taiwan data may not generalize to current economic conditions or other geographies without retraining
    \item \textbf{Feature Limitations:} Missing potentially important features (income, employment history, credit bureau scores, existing debt obligations)
    \item \textbf{Static Thresholds:} Current threshold optimization is one-time; dynamic adjustment based on economic indicators could improve robustness
    \item \textbf{Fairness Analysis:} While demographic features show low importance, comprehensive disparate impact analysis is needed for regulatory approval
    \item \textbf{Cost Parameter Uncertainty:} LGD (50\%) and profit margin (10\%) assumptions require validation with real business data; sensitivity analysis recommended
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Practical Recommendations:}

For financial institutions implementing this system:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Deploy with the three-tier decision framework (auto-approve/manual review/auto-reject) to balance automation and risk control
    \item Prioritize SHAP-guided manual review for borderline cases (5-20\% default probability range)
    \item Monitor default rate among approved customers closely; if exceeding 15\%, lower threshold immediately
    \item Retrain quarterly with new data to maintain model relevance
    \item Conduct regular fairness audits to ensure compliance with fair lending regulations
\end{enumerate}

This project validates that machine learning can generate substantial business value (\$5.75M profit) while maintaining transparency and fairness, serving as a template for responsible AI deployment in financial services.



